<h1>TActiveTextBox</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveTextBox" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActiveTextBox</tt> is the active control counterpart of
<a href="?page=Controls.TextBox">TTextBox</a>. It renders the same input and adds the
ability to change its value during a callback.
</p>

<h2>What updates during a callback</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Assigning <tt>Text</tt> writes the new value into the input in the browser, subject to
<a href="?page=ActiveControls.Introduction#ClientSideUpdates">the five conditions for a client-side update</a>. The other text box properties, including <tt>TextMode</tt> and
<tt>ReadOnly</tt>, are fixed once the control has rendered.
</p>

<h2>Calling back when the value changes</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Unlike most controls in this chapter, <tt>AutoPostBack</tt> <a href="?page=ActiveControls.Introduction#AutoPostBackDefaults">defaults to false</a> here, because
the property is inherited from <tt>TTextBox</tt> unchanged. A text box that should reach
the server when the user leaves the field has to say so.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TActiveTextBox AutoPostBack="true"
    OnTextChanged="textChanged" OnCallback="callbackRequested" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
With <tt>AutoPostBack</tt> on, leaving the field after a change starts a callback that
raises <tt>OnTextChanged</tt> first and <tt>OnCallback</tt> second. A handler that runs
for every change belongs on <tt>OnTextChanged</tt>, and one that runs only for callbacks
belongs on <tt>OnCallback</tt>.
</p>
